      <chapter xml:id="chapter-functions"><title>Functions and Graphs</title><objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol><li><p>Define a function using an input-output rule.</p></li><li><p>Distinguish data points from a mathematical model.</p></li></ol></objectives><introduction><p>Functions describe relationships between quantities and help us investigate real data.</p></introduction>
              <section xml:id="functions-intro"><title>Introduction</title>
                <p>Do countries with higher female literacy rates tend to have fewer births per woman? We can explore this question using real data.</p>
                <p>Each point on the graph represents a country. Select a point to see its name, its measurements and the year. The blue line models the general trend.</p>
                <figure xml:id="figure-literacy-fertility">
                  <caption>Female literacy and fertility: explore country-level World Bank data.</caption>
                  <interactive xml:id="interactive-literacy-fertility" iframe="https://ronandownes.github.io/book/interactives/fertility-literacy.html" width="100%" aspect="3:4"/>
                </figure>
                <p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/book/interactives/fertility-literacy.html" visual="Open the interactive graph full screen"/> for classroom projection.</p>
                <p><em>Investigate.</em> Describe the general pattern. Find two countries with similar literacy rates but different fertility rates. Click a country with fertility below 2 births per woman, and then try to find one below 1. Why might these thresholds matter? Is either threshold necessarily critical?</p>
                <p><em>Think further.</em> Does this graph demonstrate that literacy causes fertility to fall? What other factors could influence the relationship?</p>
                <p>The red points are observations, not necessarily the graph of a function. The fitted blue line <em>is</em> a function: it gives a modelled output for each literacy-rate input.</p>
                <p>Source: <url href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SE.ADT.LITR.FE.ZS" visual="World Bank/UNESCO female adult literacy"/> and <url href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.DYN.TFRT.IN" visual="World Bank total fertility"/>. The app pairs both measurements for the most recent shared year available for each country from 2015 onwards. Years differ between countries.</p>
                <p>A function assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain.</p>
              </section>
            </chapter>

    </part>

<part xml:id="part-jc-statistics">
      <title>Statistics and Probability</title>
      <xi:include href="./jc-statistics-outcomes.ptx" />
<chapter xml:id="chapter-probability">
      <title>Sample Spaces</title>
      <introduction>
        <p><em>What can happen?</em> Before calculating probabilities, we need to identify the possible outcomes of an experiment.</p>
      </introduction>
      <section xml:id="sample-spaces-learning-outcome">
        <title>Learning Outcome</title>
        <p><em>(a)</em> Generate a sample space for an experiment in a systematic way, including tree diagrams for successive events and two-way tables for independent events.</p>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="sample-spaces-intentions">
        <title>Learning Intentions and Prerequisites</title>
        <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Distinguish deterministic experiments from random (stochastic) experiments.</p></li>
            <li><p>Identify outcomes and write sample spaces using set notation.</p></li>
            <li><p>Generate sample spaces systematically using lists, tree diagrams and two-way tables.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain when each representation is useful.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </objectives>
        <p><em>Prerequisites.</em> You should be able to count systematically, multiply whole numbers, read tables and recognise simple sets. Ordered pairs are helpful but will be explained when needed.</p>
        <p><em>For later probability calculations:</em> revise adding, multiplying and simplifying fractions, and entering fractions on your calculator. These skills are not needed to begin listing outcomes.</p>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="sample-spaces-experiments">
        <title>Random and Deterministic Experiments</title>
        <p><em>Choose an input.</em> Click a number to square it. Try the same number again. Does the answer ever change?</p>
        <figure xml:id="figure-square-number"><caption>Choose a number. The rule always gives the same output for the same input.</caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-square-number" iframe="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/functions/square-a-number/index.html" width="100%" aspect="4:1"/>
        </figure>
        <p>Now roll the die. Its <term>sample space</term> is the set of all six possible results, <m>S=\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}</m>. Which one will come up next? Try it.</p>
        <figure xml:id="figure-roll-a-die"><caption>Roll the die and watch the sequence of results grow.</caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-roll-a-die" iframe="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/probability/roll-a-die/index.html" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
        </figure>
        <p>One number always has the same square. Rolling a die is different: the next result is uncertain, even though we know the six possibilities. Tossing a coin is random too: it can land heads or tails.</p>
        <example xml:id="example-random-deterministic"><title>Predict the Output</title>
          <p><em>Predict:</em> What happens when we square <m>4</m>? What might happen when we roll a die?</p>
          <p><em>Try:</em> Square <m>4</m> twice. Then roll a die several times. Record what happens.</p>
          <p><em>Notice:</em> Squaring <m>4</m> always gives <m>16</m>. The die may give different numbers. We can list the possible results, but cannot be certain which will appear next.</p>
        </example>
        <definition xml:id="definition-deterministic-experiment"><title>Deterministic Experiment</title><statement><p>A <term>deterministic experiment</term> has a predictable result when we know the starting conditions and the rule being used. The same input, under the same conditions, gives the same output.</p></statement></definition>
        <definition xml:id="definition-random-experiment"><title>Random (Stochastic) Experiment</title><statement><p>In a <term>random experiment</term>, we cannot be certain what the next result will be. We may still know all the possible results. <term>Stochastic</term> is another mathematical word for random.</p></statement></definition>
        <p><em>A little history.</em> People have played games of chance for thousands of years. In the seventeenth century, mathematicians including Blaise Pascal and Pierre de Fermat studied questions about chance. Their ideas helped develop modern probability.</p>
        <p><em>Certain or random?</em> If today is Monday, tomorrow is Tuesday: there is no uncertainty about the next day of the week. In probability language, a certain event has probability <m>1</m>. Probability <m>1</m> is valid; it simply means the event is certain.</p>
        <p><em>Does random mean 50–50?</em> No. A fair coin has two equally likely outcomes, heads and tails. But two possible outcomes do not always have equal chances. Think of whether it will rain tomorrow: rain and no rain are two possibilities, but that does not mean each has a 50% chance.</p>
        <p><em>Remember:</em> Random does not mean that anything is possible. A standard six-sided die cannot show a nine.</p>
        <p><em>What are we generating?</em> Each time you roll the die, you add another result to a <term>sequence</term>. The sequence records both the order of the rolls and any repeated numbers.</p>
        <example xml:id="example-roll-sequence-set"><title>A Sequence Is Not the Same as a Set</title>
          <p>Suppose seven rolls give <m>2,2,3,3,4,6,1</m>. This is a sequence of seven results. The corresponding set of observed values is <m>A=\{1,2,3,4,6\}</m>. In a set, each value is written only once, and order does not matter.</p>
          <p>Why must a result repeat if we roll a six-sided die seven times? There are only six different faces, so seven results cannot all be different. At least one face must occur more than once.</p>
          <p>We <em>can</em> turn the observed results into a set, but then we lose the order and the repetitions. The sample space for a single roll remains <m>S=\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}</m>, even if some numbers have not yet appeared.</p>
        </example>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="sample-spaces-definition">
        <title>Outcomes and Sample Spaces</title>
        <definition xml:id="definition-outcome"><title>Outcome</title><statement><p>An <term>outcome</term> is one possible result of an experiment.</p></statement></definition>
        <definition xml:id="definition-sample-space"><title>Sample Space</title><statement><p>The <term>sample space</term> is the set of all possible outcomes of a random experiment. We often denote it by <m>S</m>.</p></statement></definition>
        <example xml:id="example-coin-once"><title>Tossing a Coin</title><p>Let <m>H</m> mean heads and <m>T</m> mean tails. The sample space for one toss is <m>S=\{H,T\}</m>.</p></example>
        <example xml:id="example-die-once"><title>Rolling a Die</title><p>For one roll of a standard six-sided die, <m>S=\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}</m>.</p></example>
        <example xml:id="example-card-once"><title>Make a Playing Card from Two Random Numbers</title>
          <p>A die has one property that we record: its face value. A playing card has two properties that identify it: <term>value</term> and <term>suit</term>. We do not need a real deck to investigate the 52 possible cards.</p>
          <p>Generate the first random integer from <m>1</m> to <m>13</m>. It represents the card's value: <m>1</m> is ace, <m>2</m> through <m>10</m> are numbered cards, <m>11</m> is jack, <m>12</m> is queen, and <m>13</m> is king.</p>
          <p>Generate a second random integer from <m>1</m> to <m>4</m>. In alphabetical order the suits are <m>1</m> clubs, <m>2</m> diamonds, <m>3</m> hearts, and <m>4</m> spades.</p>
          <p>For example, the ordered pair <m>(7,3)</m> means the seven of hearts. The first number always gives the value; the second always gives the suit.</p>
          <figure xml:id="figure-card-random-pair"><caption>Generate two random numbers to create a playing card.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-card-random-pair" iframe="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/probability/card-from-two-numbers/index.html" width="100%" aspect="3:1"/>
          </figure>
          <p>There are <m>13</m> choices for the value and <m>4</m> for the suit. Each pair identifies one card, giving <m>13\cdot4=52</m> possible outcomes. A two-way table could show the 13 values across its columns and the four suits down its rows.</p>
          <p><em>Investigate:</em> Generate ten cards. Can the same card appear again? Why? This app samples <em>with replacement</em>: it generates each pair afresh rather than removing a card from a physical deck.</p>
          <p><em>Calculator challenge:</em> If your calculator can generate random integers, make one from 1 to 13 and another from 1 to 4. Translate the pair into a card. The exact key sequence depends on your calculator model.</p>
          <p>The two generated numbers are independent in this model. Card colour is different: it is determined by suit, so colour and suit are not independent.</p>
        </example>
        <p>The outcomes in a sample space need not be equally likely.</p>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="sample-spaces-trees">
        <title>Successive Stages and Tree Diagrams</title>
        <definition xml:id="definition-tree-diagram"><title>Tree Diagram</title><statement><p>A <term>tree diagram</term> is a branching diagram showing possible outcomes at successive stages of an experiment.</p></statement></definition>
        <example xml:id="example-two-tosses"><title>Two Coin Tosses</title>
          <p>Toss a coin twice. On the first toss we may get <m>H</m> or <m>T</m>. Each of these branches splits into <m>H</m> or <m>T</m> on the second toss.</p>
          <pre>Start
  H
    H  → HH
    T  → HT
  T
    H  → TH
    T  → TT</pre>
          <p>Hence <m>S=\{HH,HT,TH,TT\}</m>. The order matters: <m>HT</m> and <m>TH</m> are different outcomes.</p>
        </example>
        <p>Tree diagrams are useful even when successive stages are dependent, such as drawing cards without replacement.</p>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="sample-spaces-two-way">
        <title>Two-Way Tables</title>
        <definition xml:id="definition-two-way-table"><title>Two-Way Table</title><statement><p>A <term>two-way table</term> organises outcomes by two variables, characteristics or stages.</p></statement></definition>
        <example xml:id="example-two-tosses-table"><title>Two Coin Tosses in a Table</title>
          <table><title>First toss (rows) and second toss (columns)</title><tabular>
            <row><cell>First / Second</cell><cell>H</cell><cell>T</cell></row>
            <row><cell>H</cell><cell>HH</cell><cell>HT</cell></row>
            <row><cell>T</cell><cell>TH</cell><cell>TT</cell></row>
          </tabular></table>
          <p>The table gives the same sample space as the tree diagram: <m>S=\{HH,HT,TH,TT\}</m>.</p>
        </example>
        <example xml:id="example-card-classification"><title>One Card, Two Characteristics</title>
          <p>Choose one card. We may classify it by suit (hearts, diamonds, clubs or spades) and rank (ace, 2 through 10, jack, queen or king). A two-way table would have four suit rows and thirteen rank columns, giving one distinct card in each cell.</p>
        </example>
        <p>A two-way table does not require independent events: it can represent dependent or independent events. Independence concerns probabilities, not the shape of the table. We will study this distinction later.</p>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="sample-spaces-practice">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise xml:id="exercise-sample-spaces-classify"><statement><p>Classify each as deterministic or random: (a) calculating <m>9+4</m>; (b) rolling a die; (c) selecting a card from a shuffled deck without looking.</p></statement><answer><p>(a) Deterministic; (b) random; (c) random.</p></answer></exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="exercise-sample-spaces-three-tosses"><statement><p>List the sample space for three successive coin tosses. Check that you have not omitted any outcomes.</p></statement><answer><p><m>S=\{HHH,HHT,HTH,HTT,THH,THT,TTH,TTT\}</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="exercise-sample-spaces-two-dice"><statement><p>Draw a two-way table for rolling two standard dice. How many ordered outcomes are possible?</p></statement><answer><p>Six rows and six columns give 36 ordered outcomes.</p></answer></exercise>
      </section>
    </chapter>
    </part>

